So originally, the plan was to talk more about reinforcement learning. But I thought since I only had one lecture to give, I think this class would be a little bit incomplete without saying something about multimodality, which is so pervasive if you look at all the major models. So this will be an overview of multimodal models. Of course, this could be a whole class in itself, and I'll try to do what I can in one lecture. OK, so let's dive into multimodal models. So far in this class, we have exclusively focused on language models. And language models are already pretty general. They give you the ability to go from any piece of text to any text. So this could be any language, code, poetry, anything you like, even, let's say, DNA, for example. But the world is multi-modal. You have text, you have images, audio, video. And if you think about a North Star of where we want to be is what people call omni model, which is ability to take any combination of these modalities and output any combination of any of these modalities. So, for example, I can give you an image and a video and I can ask a question about the image and the video. I can generate images. I can convert the audio into an image. You can imagine all sorts of different applications by having an omni model. So in this class, I'm not going to build up to a whole omni model. But I'll give you some of the pieces that at least to allow you to ingest a little bit of images, which we'll mostly focus on today. So how do you build such a thing? Well, this is a weird way to motivate this, but the reality is that transformers work really well. And despite the best efforts of people to try other things, those are across all of these modalities still at scale the best thing we have. So we have to figure out how to use them. And now, transformers were designed for text. So they have this property that they speak tokens. So they take as input a bunch of tokens and then output some tokens. And here, I'm expanding the notion 