t is not really prompting the unusualness, it still talks about it, whereas-- GPT-4, obviously, is able to do this, but other models at the time were not able to. OK, any questions about LLaVA? OK, so that was 2023. And I'm going to skip forward to LLaVA OneVision, which is 2024. There is a sequence of papers after the LLaVA paper came out of LLaVA 1.5, LLaVA-Next. But I'll capture all of these innovations, I think, in what I'll describe next. The main thing that they did is it's essentially the same recipe, but they are trying to be more ambitious in terms of the types of multimodal applications they could do. So they were able to handle multiple images and videos. So here's the same diagram. Now they can take a single image or multiple images or a video, which is essentially a sequence of images corresponding to a sampling of the frames. And here are the vision encoder. They upgrade it to SigLIP. The text decoder, they use Qwen-2 now, which was probably the best language model that was out at that time. And then for the projector or the adapter, which is the thing that takes the output of the vision encoder and turns it into the input of the language model is a two-layer MLP instead of a linear projection. OK, so it's like you have a system and you're just upgrading the parts. But it's the same rough system. So let's talk a little bit about data processing. So one thing that they wanted to do is to do OCR. And the thing with OCR is that you need to preserve very fine-grained information. Otherwise, a J looks like an I, and that's not good. So remember that CLIP resizes and crops to 336 by 336. And clearly, if you have a document and you crop to 336 by 336, you can't read it. So their solution is this idea called AnyRes, which was actually introduced in the LLaVA 1.5 paper. And the idea is basically, I mean, it's fairly straightforward. You break up an image into multiple pieces. And the idea is that each piece is the resolution of whatever the vision encoder is going to take, 336 by 336, for example. So you're going to code all the pieces into a bunch of vectors. And then you concatenate the vectors. OK, so you're basically noticing that your vision encoder can't handle high resolution. So rather than downsampling, you're just going to crop and look at different parts of image. And now if you have really super high resolution images or videos then, you get too many tokens. So then they downsample. But the idea behind AnyRes is that it's adaptive. And this is nice because the transformer is already adaptive. Language can be-- your sentences can be any length and the transformer already handles that pretty well. So it turns out images can be any resolution. So that's picking back on the same dynamic ability. So here is what they actually do for images here. So let's say you have this image of a paper. They do downsample and have one path, which is basically a downsampled encoding of the entire paper. But then they break it up into these chunks, where each chunk is the resolution that the vision encoder expects, and then encodes each of these separately. And then you concatenate all these patches. And if you have too many, then you reduce that the number of patches by interpolation. OK, so then they're able to handle images, multiple images, and video. So you think about, well, all of these are technically all reducible to images, but they put their thumb on the scale a little bit because they want to make sure all the modalities are roughly comparable. Because videos can be very long, they don't want their data set to be dominated by a bunch of repetitive frames. So here's what they do here. So for a single image, they basically have the full image downsampled and then a bunch of crops up to N crops there, up to 9. And if you are presented with multiple images, then they just use the base resolution. So if you have a single image, I get to look at it more carefully. If I have multiple images, I'm just going to look at it from 