emember, OpenCLIP was the open reproductions of the CLIP model. So it's basically a CLIP encoder. And for the adapter, they did one layer of cross-attention, incorporate 2D positional embeddings and maps to a fixed size of 256, which is a little bit-- I guess this will be changed later because this is definitely not very dynamic, but neither is the vision encoder at this point. I guess this got cut off. But the special tokens, they have an image tag and a box tag for bounding box, and a ref tag for descriptions. This is what happens when your slides are in HTML. So for training, they have three stages, just like with the LLaVA models. In the first stage, the idea is to-- I mean, they call it pre-training but you're not pre-training from scratch. This is generally large-scale, low quality data. They freeze the language model and train the vision encoder and adapter. So this is a bit different from the LLaVA models, in that, they train the vision encoder at this point. And you can see some of the data sets that they are training on, 1.4 billion examples. Stage 2, they move to higher quality task-specific data. And you can see some of the usual suspects of VQA data sets, chart question answering, and so on. And then they train all the parameters here. And finally, stage 3 is instruction tuning data. You freeze the visual encoder and you train the adapter and the language model. Here are some examples of the type of things that they're able to do. So, of course, this is Qwen. So some of these examples are in Chinese. Their interest is also making a model that is supposed to be subsuming a language model. So a language model can do code. So we want the vision model to be good at code as well. And you can have this example, if you can see it, is an image. And can you find Spider-Man and the Hulk. And it can output bounding boxes as well. It doesn't actually output an image. It just outputs a bounding box. And then you can do OCR. You can compare-- so usual stuff. OK, so then Qwen-2 was, again, a upgrade. There's some new ideas here, which I'll go through. So use a larger vision encoder. They also started doing dynamic resolution. I think this was the main thing. We remember when we went from LLaVA to LLaVA OneVision, they realized, oh, we need to handle images of different sizes. And if you try to do video, it's clear that you need some dynamic resolution. So the model is basically-- so let's say you have this picture here, that might be mapped to 11,000 tokens. This tiny picture of an equation might be only mapped to 8 tokens and so on and so forth. So how do they do this? Well, basically each-- it's the same idea AnyRes idea from LLaVA, where each 224 by 224 patch is encoded with a VIT. And remember the VIT that they started with is the OpenCLIP bit. But this gets fine-tuned. And then trying to reduce the contact size, they compress every 2 by 2 into 1 so that they ultimately edge every patch generates 66 tokens. For eight videos, they sample 2 frames a second but max out at 16,000 tokens. OK, so one thing coming back to this question about positional embeddings, so they did do something different here with Qwen-2. So they used this idea called multimodal rotary, multimodal RoPE. So remember, we saw RoPE before. And the idea behind RoPE is that you have embedding such that the inner product between the vectors depends only on the distance. So the distance is defined in 1D by just the number of tokens. You're away from it. So now, the multidimensional version is essentially the same thing. Except for now you have 3D. So you have height. You have width and you have time. So each position, each patch now is a triple defined by the coordinates. And then to compute the M-RoPE, basically, for every dimension, you compute the RoPE and then you concatenate. So it's a fairly straightforward idea. Although, later, with Qwen-3, we'll see how this is actually a bit suboptimal. So they initialize the language model with-- this is the Qwen-VL model. So, of course, they're initializing with Gwen. And then they have three stages of training, which is very similar to what we did before. And then they were able to show a bunch of different capabilities, some video understanding. It can do math and code, function calling, and so on and so forth. OK, so quickly, I'll talk about Gwen-3. So again, there's a few changes here. I wouldn't say these are structural big changes, but they're probably changes that do impact the quality of the model. So this is from last year Gwen3-VL report. And so it's roughly the same diagram here. I'll point out maybe-- I'll come back to this diagram. I'll go through some of the changes. So one is that now they're using Qwen-3 models. They have a series of dense and MoE models. And the Qwen-3 models are really, really good, and it really helps the quality of the final model. One thing that they're vested in is long context understanding. So their context length can go up to 256K, which is really important if you're trying to do long video. Let's talk about the vision encoder. So they use SigLIP-2, which is basically an improved version of SigLIP. It's actually identical architecture, is designed to be backward compatible. And then they improved their RoPE. And the way to explain this is, before, you had the three dimensions, time, width, and height. And basically, when to basically allocate the first block of dimensions to the time, then the next block to width and the next to height. And the problem, if you remember RoPE is that each component refers represents a different frequency. So this would mean that maybe all the temporal dimensions are low frequency and all the high dimensions are high frequency. So they basically interleave them when they realize that. And they now all the axes are exposed to both low and high frequency. Another thing they did was explicit video timestamps. So if you look closely at this-- wait. OK, this is Qwen-3. Oops. So if you look-- this is probably a little bit too small. But before, the time stamp was implicit in the positional encodings. Each frame in the video intrinsically gets a different-- there's a notion of time just because it's in a different position. But here, they made the time explicit. So these are actually tokens zero seconds, is a token that is now you have a token that represents the time. And they found this to be helpful presumably because it's something that you can directly refer to, like what happened after two seconds. So that is one of the main changes. They also did this square root normalized per token loss. So the idea is that some examples that involve video are very long and some examples that involve a single image are very short. And the normal thing is that every token is treated the same. And this would mean that the video examples are going to dominate. So they basically-- the details aren't too clear. But I believe they normalized each example by the square root of the length to basically downweight the impact of really long examples. The final thing that is worth noting, which is interesting, is that they did something interesting with adapter. So remember we started the adapter is the thing that connects the vision encoder to the language model. And LLaVA was the simplest. It was just a linear projection. And then we got an MLP. And then we got some cross-attention. And DeepStack, which is actually a paper from the DeepSeek team, but when used here is more sophisticated. They noticed that, well, the vision encoder already computes a stack of vision embeddings. And we're basically going to basically add these directly into the residual stream of the language model. OK, so this is a bit more of a deep fusion of the vision encoder into the language model as opposed to the vision encoder is a black box that just outputs a sequence of vectors. OK, so training is-- so they have two phases. Pre-training has actually four stages. Post-training has three stages. So you can see that this pipelines are getting quite complicated now. So they first train the adapter. This is the same as all of these models. And then they have three stages where they progressively train on longer and longer sequences, from 8K, to 32K, to 256K. Most of the tokens are in the stages 2 and 3. And then for post-training, they do SFT on long chain of thought data, knowledge distillation, and then some reinforcement learning. At this point, it's really a systems paper. There's a bunch of-- I highlighted the core new ideas, but there's a lot of details that are different. And if you look at the final results, this is a pretty good model. I mean, if you look at these benchmarks as compared to the closed models Gemini, GPT-5, and Opus 4.1, these numbers are-- the bold means that it's the best number in that row. So the Qwen models are actually quite strong. OK, maybe the-- yeah, a question. [INAUDIBLE] Yeah, so the question is what about video generation and how do you know whether there should be a video or text? So first of all, these models don't generate videos or images. All the multimodal stuff is on the input side. You're always generating text. And then you're always in most of these stages, except for RL, you're supervising every token. So there's no LLM as a judge or quality on how well the description is working. It's just basically whatever is in your data set. Now, with RL, you can play around with different rewards. Yeah. I'll ask is the training of multimodal models harder than training a pure LLM from a system perspective? [INAUDIBLE] Yeah, so the two questions are trained multimodal models harder on the system side? And, I mean, certainly, it's not easier. I think you pointed out that the data sets are larger. Video data is just-- even loading it can be a bottleneck. Whereas generally, we have not really focused on data loading for talking about language models because it's very, very cheap. So that needs to be taken into account. And all the same principles of making sure that your data loading is happening async with actual computation needs to be taken into account. And the second part was-- sorry, what was the second question? [INAUDIBLE] Oh, yeah. So the videos and images can have more-- especially video have more tokens. So that's one reason we saw this normalization to make sure that we're not giving more weight to video. I mean, you can always down weight. So if you have more tokens, if you don't like that, you can down weight. We talked in the last or two weeks ago about data mixtures. So you can always weight things according to whatever makes sense. But there's also a lot of text tokens out there. So I think, in general, if you look at, yeah, the models are trained on tens of trillions of tokens. I think there's a-- yeah, I wouldn't say that the number of multimodal tokens vastly outnumbers text tokens. Yeah, oh, I think you were first. [INAUDIBLE] So the question is, when you do this alignment, how does it know what the language-- is the model language model pre-trained? And it definitely has to be pre-trained because otherwise it doesn't make sense to align it. So the language model is frozen. And you're just training the adapter to connect the given vision encoder with a given language model. And the way you train it is, well, you pick a token budget, 67 billion tokens and you just train. There's not an adaptive threshold here. Yeah. [INAUDIBLE] Yeah, so the question is, is the number of parameters in the vision coder much smaller? And the answer is yes, in general. I'm trying to find-- where is the other place I-- not here. OK, the reason is that the vision encoder is in some sense doing a very local operation. It's looking at a patch. A patch is very small. And it's just trying to understand the patches. There's not much knowledge. So the patch, we're not reasoning. So most of the capabilities of the model are still in the language model. I'm trying to find the-- OK, where? Maybe it was up here. OK, I can't-- oh, yeah. So if you look at the LLaVA model, so the full-- so 72 billion parameter language model. Sorry. What-- OK, I don't know why this number is so-- oh, sorry. This is 72 million, OK. So this is much smaller. The projector is much smaller. And the VIT is generally less than a billion, parameters. Yeah, I guess mostly because it's fairly a local operation. I guess that's the easy way to say it. OK, I'm going to think some other questions. I'm going to try to move on now. So I Qwen-3 is the last vision language model. I'll talk about this SOTA, Save Our Performance. There's a lot of data work that happens. Not too many details about the data and the data mix in these later Qwen models. So you'll have to look at the LLaVA paper or the-- AI2 has a [INAUDIBLE] paper, which I didn't get a chance to talk about that has more details here. And from Qwen to Qwen-2, Qwen-3, many of these changes were mostly-- there's some changes there. But the overall framework remains the same. And mostly, it's scaling up, curating more data sets, noticing that your context isn't-- you have to handle long context and maybe sharpening your image processing and so on. OK, so I'm going to now jump to something quite different. So there's this paper called Chameleon from Meta in 2024. So, so far, VLMs encode images into these vectors and then they inject them into a language model. And because it's a language model, you can only generate text. You can't generate images. So there's many ways you can go about fixing this. You can just take a VLM. You can also attach a diffusion head. And now you can generate images or-- but I want to talk about this idea here, which is Chameleon says, what if we mapped everything into discrete tokens? And in some ways aesthetically this is-- well, maybe this reflects that I'm a language person. This is appealing because now you can analyze and generate images in the same way. Everything is a discrete token. If you want to do what-- let's say you want to generate images. You can say, here's a text prompt. And then you can just-- these are more just tokens that you can generate. Or you can flip the language and the text around as well. They have these examples from the Chameleon paper you can prompt in say I'm bored. Can you show me some birds? And then it would be text, and then it would be images, and then some more text and some images. So it's interleaved. So in some sense, the vision of an omni model is that text and images truly live in the same space. And this is accomplished by making everything look like text. That's one way to do it. OK, so I'll talk a little bit about how this is done. So the thing you need to do now is to map images into discrete tokens. So there's this idea rather old from Oord 2017, which is called vector quantized variational autoencoder. And the basic idea here is to map an image into a discrete code. And what you learn is a mapping that maps this into a continuous vector. And this gets rounded to the nearest code. So you have a codebook which has, let's say, 8,000 codes. So these are prototypical vectors that correspond to what patches. And then so you pick one of these that's the closest. And that would be your representation. So that's your encoder. It goes through a continuous phase and then you round it to the nearest code. And now the decoder is you take that code and you try to reconstruct the image. And then you train these VQ-VAEs by basically minimizing the reconstruction loss. There are some other terms that you add because this is not differentiable, which I won't have time to get into. OK, but, at the end of the day, you have 512 by 512 images that are converted into 1,024 tokens, and each token comes from a vocabulary of 8,000. OK, so now you have basically text. You have your images, which are basically these codes. They train a new tokenizer because now you have different-- your data looks different than if it were just normal natural language. The training is now actually very straightforward. This is just normal language model training. There's no adapter. There's no turn the vision encoder. There's just a language model now. So this is the part that's appealing. So there's still two stages. But even for language model training, there's usually two or more stages, where the first stage is the bulk of training. It's largely unsupervised. They have both text and text and image. And then stage 2, they mix in high quality data. OK, so this is really great and elegant. There's a few problems with this. One is that they found that the training was not stable. And the reason is that text and images, despite occupying the same space, just behave very differently. So just calling things discrete tokens isn't hiding the fact that there's an image living there. And in particular, text tokens, if you think about predicting the next word, this has relatively lower entropy. Most words are predictable, whereas image tokens have very high entropy. I don't know what shade of blue this exact token is going to be. And so as a result, they found that training with this mixture led to a lot of the norms of the parameters to grow and to generate loss instability. They were able to mitigate or fix this to some extent via QK norm and z-loss regularization, which controls the norm growth. So I'm not even going to show the results. But I wanted to highlight this work because there's a certain elegance here because it's just one model that treats really all modalities the same. But the downside is that it turned out this model was not really as performant, and the discretization does definitely lose its information. So think about OCR. Again, if you discretize very small print, you're not going to be able to read it anymore. And finally, training with multi modalities is also tricky. We saw this a little bit with the Qwen models and we had to play around with the weights. But this is more exacerbated here. So VQ-VAEs were popular for a while. And in fact, many people were using this for image generation. The main reason to do that is, well, you have a transformer. How do you generate from a transformer? Well, you have to generate discrete things and you basically put all your data into this discrete form. But then diffusion models came out and became popular and viable for generation. So this flavor of method is less popular than it used to be. OK, so maybe just reflect and summarize here. Frontier models these days are expected to be multimodal or even more strongly natively multimodal or omni models. Unfortunately, I think when Gemini comes out or GPT comes out, they tout or touted as being natively multimodal and handle all these modalities. And in fact, they do. But of course, there's no details about how these are built. I think that it's probably combination of having a continuous encoder because you don't want to lose information and then diffusion for the generation because-- but that's my speculation. The fundamental challenge, I think, when you're dealing with multimodality is how do you handle non-text modalities? It's also interesting that there's a asymmetry between understanding the modality and generation. And there's no one universal encoder let's say. For example, when we looked at CLIP, you only cared for classification to capture high level semantics. So these vectors could be fairly small and capture the high level semantics. Whereas if you wanted to do OCR or whether you wanted to generate an image, then you need really fine-grained detail. You need to-- that's why diffusion is so good because it can really micro optimize the low-high frequency information. I think, in general, when you're dealing with multiple modalities, you have to think carefully about weighing them properly. For example, video certainly has lower information density than text. So you don't want a video to overwhelm your text. And like I said before, at least the current best thing is having continuous encoders. It seems even CLIP, even though it's five years old, or similar ideas are still the go-to way to capture semantics of images. Transformers are still there. And then diffusion models, which I didn't talk about, are great for generation. OK, so I will stop there. That's it for the lecture on multimodality. I guess we don't have any homework on doing this. But if you're curious, I think I encourage you to play around with training some of these models.


